  "enhanced_information": "The patient presents with symptoms consistent with hyperthyroidism, including nausea, vomiting, palpitations, and diarrhea, alongside a heart rate of 145/min. The laboratory results show a low TSH level (0.28 mIU/L) and an elevated Total T4 level (12 µg/dL), indicating thyroid hormone excess. To accurately assess the patient's thyroid function, measuring Free T4 levels is crucial, as it reflects the active hormone status more reliably than Total T4, especially in pregnancy. Other tests such as Total T3 or TBG levels are less relevant in this context. Therefore, the most useful additional laboratory test for this patient is Free thyroxine (T4) levels.",
  "analysis": "The patient presents with symptoms indicative of hyperthyroidism, including nausea, vomiting, palpitations, and diarrhea, along with a heart rate of 145/min. The laboratory results show a low TSH level (0.28 mIU/L) and an elevated Total T4 level (12 µg/dL), which confirms the suspicion of hyperthyroidism. In pregnancy, it is essential to assess the levels of Free T4 rather than Total T4, as Free T4 provides a more accurate reflection of the biologically active thyroid hormone. Total T4 can be influenced by changes in thyroid-binding proteins during pregnancy, making Free T4 a more reliable indicator of thyroid function. Therefore, measuring Free T4 levels is the most useful additional laboratory test in this scenario to confirm the diagnosis and guide management. Other options, such as Total T3 or TBG levels, are less relevant in this context, and tests for antibodies (TPO or TRAb) are not immediately necessary for the assessment of hyperthyroidism in this case. Thus, the best-supported option is Free thyroxine (T4) levels.",
